---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
import Image from "../components/EditorialImage.astro";
import ProductImage from "../components/ProductImage.astro";
import products from "../data/products.json";
import { photosFor } from "../data/photography";
const lead = products.find(product => product.slug === 'nonna-s-sunday-supper-society-c1717-ivory')!;
// The manifesto lives here; /manifesto/ redirects to #manifesto (scripts/finalize.mjs).
const tenets = [
 ["Wear it your way.", "A graphic tee with worn denim. Fleece over a favorite shirt. Start with a piece you like and make it yours."],
 ["Check the fit.", "A boxy tee and a classic crewneck wear differently. Each product page shows the cut, fabric and finished-garment measurements."],
 ["Look closer.", "Small chest marks, full back prints and embroidered cuffs. The detail belongs to the piece, with four garment views before you choose."],
 ["Keep it in rotation.", "Follow the care label, wash prints inside out where permitted, and choose a fit you’ll want to wear again."],
];
---

<Site
  title="The story"
  description="Independent graphic clothing, established in 2011. Discover the original tees, the black-sheep mark and the fabric and fit behind the current pieces."
>
  <section class="page-head shell about-opening"><div><p class="label">The story · Est. 2011</p><h1>STARTED WITH<br />A T-SHIRT.</h1><p class="lede">Soft cotton. A graphic worth wearing. A fit you come back to.</p><p>After Hours Agenda started in Phoenix, Arizona, in 2011. David Marsh made shirts for friends, then packed a suitcase and drove to events, asking people to feel how soft they were.</p><p>That attention to the piece still leads the collection: cotton tees, fleece layers and ribbed knitwear, with a different graphic and cut for each.</p><a class="text-link" href="/shop/">Find a piece of your own</a></div><figure class="about-photo"><a href={`/shop/${lead.slug}/`}><ProductImage image={photosFor(lead)[1] ?? photosFor(lead)[0]!} priority sizes="(max-width:760px) 100vw, 45vw" /></a><figcaption>{lead.title} · {lead.color} · AI-generated campaign</figcaption></figure></section>
  <section class="shell archive-panel"><figure><Image name="2012-the-prologue" alt="The typed January 2012 Prologue from the original brand archive." /><figcaption>The original Prologue · January 2012 · Brand archive</figcaption></figure><div><p class="label">From the beginning</p><h2>WE CREATE.<br />WE BUILD.</h2><p>In January 2012, the first site opened with a typed page called The Prologue. “We are dreamers,” it said. “We create, and we build.”</p><p>The original tees and the black-sheep mark are part of that history. The archive keeps those early pieces available to see.</p><a class="text-link" href="/lookbook/">See the clothes in context</a></div></section>
  <section class="shell section founder" id="founder" aria-labelledby="founder-title">
    <div>
      <p class="label">The founder</p>
      <h2 id="founder-title">
        IT STARTED
        <br />
        <span class="accent">WITH A SUITCASE.</span>
      </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="founder-body">
      <p class="lede">
        After Hours Agenda is David Marsh’s clothing label, from Phoenix,
        Arizona.
      </p>
      <p>
        David started it in 2011 as a hobby alongside a full-time job, with no
        plan beyond making shirts worth being proud of. The first ones went to
        friends. Then came a suitcase and a car: David drove to any event within
        reach, handing people shirts and asking them to feel how soft they were.
      </p>
      <p>
        Orders started coming in from across the country, then from other
        countries. A customer in Austria wrote three paragraphs about the
        quality, the fit and the message of the shirt. One day David’s phone
        filled with messages: one of the shirts was on national TV.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="founder-quote">
        <p>
          “If you consistently drive a great product, you will have a success
          story. You just have to be patient.”
        </p>
        <footer>David Marsh · <cite>Hit the Ground Running</cite>, 2013</footer>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        That’s still how it works here: make each piece well, listen to the
        people who wear it, and keep going.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="shell section manifesto" id="manifesto" aria-labelledby="manifesto-title">
    <div class="manifesto-head">
      <div>
        <p class="label">The manifesto</p>
        <h2 id="manifesto-title">
          MADE TO WEAR.
          <br />
          <span class="accent">YOUR WAY.</span>
        </h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Start with the garment. Choose the graphic, cut and color you want to wear.
        </p>
      </div>
      <img src="/brand/sheep.svg" width="220" height="173" alt="" loading="lazy" />
    </div>
    {tenets.map(([title, text], index) => (
      <div class="values">
        <span class="label">0{index + 1}</span>
        <h3>{title}</h3>
        <p>{text}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </section>
</Site>
